    <statement><p>For each of the following vector fields, find its curl and determine
if it is a gradient field.</p>
<p><alert>(a)</alert>
<m>\vec F = 2\mathopen{}\left(xy+z^{2}\right)\,\mathit{\vec i}+4\mathopen{}\left(x^{2}+yz\right)\,\mathit{\vec j}+4\mathopen{}\left(xz+y^{2}\right)\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>:</p>
<p><m>\mbox{curl }\vec F =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>\vec F</m> <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0002"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>is a gradient field</p>
</li><li>
<p>is not a gradient field</p>
</li></ul></p>
<p><alert>(b)</alert>
<m>\vec G = \left(4xz+y^{2}\right)\,\mathit{\vec i}+2xy\,\mathit{\vec j}+2x^{2}\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>:</p>
<p><m>\mbox{curl }\vec G =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><m>\vec G</m> <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0004"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>is a gradient field</p>
</li><li>
<p>is not a gradient field</p>
</li></ul></p>
<p><alert>(c)</alert>
<m>\vec H = \left(2xy+yz\right)\,\mathit{\vec i}+\left(x^{2}+z^{2}\right)\,\mathit{\vec j}+2xz\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>:</p>
<p><m>\mbox{curl }\vec H =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0005"/></p>
<p><m>\vec H</m> <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0006"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>is a gradient field</p>
</li><li>
<p>is not a gradient field</p>
</li></ul></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4y\,\mathit{\vec i}+6x\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{is not a gradient field}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\boldsymbol{0}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{is a gradient field}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-2z\,\mathit{\vec i}+\left(y-2z\right)\,\mathit{\vec j}-z\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{is not a gradient field}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert>(a)</alert>
Since <m>\mbox{curl } \vec F = 4y\,\mathit{\vec i}+6x\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>,
we know by the curl test that <m>\vec F</m>
is not a gradient field.




<alert>(b)</alert>
Since <m>\mbox{curl } \vec G = \boldsymbol{0}</m>and <m>\vec G</m> is defined everywhere
we know by the curl test that <m>\vec G</m>
is a gradient field.




<alert>(c)</alert>
Since <m>\mbox{curl } \vec H = -2z\,\mathit{\vec i}+\left(y-2z\right)\,\mathit{\vec j}-z\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>
we know by the curl test that <m>\vec H</m>
is not a gradient field.



</solution>
